  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Some stronger showers and storms possible Saturday especially \n   during the afternoon.\n\n-  Widespread showers increasing Sunday into Monday with \n   seasonably cool temperatures persisting into early next week.\n\n-  Gradual drying trend develops mid-week alongside morning \n   valley fog.\n\nA broad trough of low pressure over the Pacific Northwest will \nmaintain unseasonably cool conditions across western Montana and \nnorth-central Idaho, with valley high temperatures remaining 5 to \n10 degrees below normal in the 60s and 70s. Showers Saturday \nmorning east of US Highway 93 and across northwest Montana will \nyield to partial afternoon clearing. Solar heating will generate \nweak to modest instability, supporting spotty afternoon showers \nand thunderstorms capable of brief heavy rainfall, lightning, \nhail, and erratic convective outflow gusts of 25 to 45 mph.\n\nAn upper-level closed low will track directly across the Northern\nRockies Sunday through Monday before departing Saskatchewan-bound \nby Tuesday. Dynamic forcing and rich moisture advection will \nproduce widespread, soaking rainfall. Probabilities for rainfall \nexceeding 0.50 inches are very high between Sunday morning and \nTuesday morning reaching 80 to 90+ percent across West- Central \nand Northwest Montana, 60 to 80+ percent across North-Central \nIdaho, and as low as 40 percent for Southwest Montana. A 10 \npercent chance exists for total rainfall exceeding 2.00 inches in\nWest- Central and Northwest Montana through Tuesday morning. Cold\nair entrainment aloft will lower snow levels, producing some \nmountain snow accumulations above 9,000 feet.\n\nThe forecast becomes a bit uncertain toward the middle part of\nnext week, but what we know so far is:\nThe closed low exits to the northeast on Tuesday, allowing high\npressure to build over the region into the middle of next week.\nThis pattern will bring a drying trend with moderating\ntemperatures, though lingering boundary-layer moisture will\ncontinue to support overnight and early morning valley fog and \nlow stratus.",
